# Data Analytics Lens AWS Well-Architected Framework

> This document describes the AWS Well-Architected Data Analytics Lens, a collection of customer-proven best practices for designing well-architected analytics workloads. The Data Analytics Lens contains insights that AWS has gathered from real-world case studies, and helps you learn the key design elements of well-architected analytics workloads along with recommendations for improvement. The document is intended for IT architects, developers, and team members who build and operate analytics systems.

- [Introduction](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/analytics-lens.md)
- [Workload context checklist](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/workload-context-checklist.md)
- [Design principles by pillar](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principles-by-pillar.md)
- [Conclusion](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/conclusion.md)
- [Contributors](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/contributors.md)
- [Document revisions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/document-revisions.md)
- [Notices](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/notices.md)
- [AWS Glossary](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/glossary.md)

## [Pillars of the Well-Architected Framework](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/well-architected-design-principles.md)

### [Operational excellence](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/operational-excellence.md)

The operational excellence pillar includes the ability to support development and run workloads effectively, gain insight into your operations, and continually improve supporting processes and procedures that deliver business value.

### [1 – Monitor the health of the analytics application workload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-1.md)

Data analytics workloads often involve multiple systems and process steps working in coordination.

- [BP 1.1 – Validate the data quality of source systems before transferring data for analytics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-1.1---validate-the-data-quality-of-source-systems-before-transferring-data-for-analytics..md): Data quality can have an intrinsic impact on the success or failure of your organization’s data analytics projects.
- [BP 1.2 – Monitor operational metrics of data processing jobs and the availability of source data](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-1.2---monitor-operational-metrics-of-data-processing-jobs-and-the-availability-of-source-data..md): Data processing pipelines often consist of multiple steps that all need to run in sequence to output the desired data sets and meet business deadlines.

### [2 – Modernize deployment of the analytics jobs and applications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-2.md)

2 - Modernize deployment of the analytics jobs and applications

- [BP 2.1 – Use version control for job and application changes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-2.1---use-version-control-for-job-and-application-changes..md): Version control systems support tracking changes and the ability to revert to previous versions of an analytics system should changes cause unintended consequences.
- [BP 2.2 – Create test data and provision staging environment](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-2.2---create-test-data-and-provision-staging-environment..md): Using a known and unchanging dataset for test purposes helps ensure that when changes are made to the analytics environment or analytics application code, test results can be compared to previous versions.
- [BP 2.3 – Test and validate analytics jobs and application deployments](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-2.3---test-and-validate-analytics-jobs-and-application-deployments..md): Before making changes in production environments, use standard and repeatable automated tests to validate performance and accuracy of results.
- [BP 2.4 – Build standard operating procedures for deployment, test, rollback, and backfill tasks](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-2.4---build-standard-operating-procedures-for-deployment-test-rollback-and-backfill-tasks..md): Standard operating procedures for deployment, test, rollback, and data backfill tasks allow faster deployments, reduce the number of errors that reach production.

### [Security](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/security.md)

The security pillar encompasses the protection of data, systems, and assets to take advantage of cloud technologies to improve your security.

### [3 – Designing data platforms for governance and compliance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-3.md)

How do you protect data in your organization’s analytics workload? Privacy by Design (PbD) is an approach in system engineering that takes privacy into account throughout the whole engineering process.

- [BP 3.1 – Privacy by Design](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.1-privacy-by-design..md): Privacy by Design is an approach in system engineering that takes privacy into account throughout the whole engineering process.
- [BP 3.2 – Classify and protect data](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.2---classify-and-protect-data..md): How do you classify and protect data in analytics workload? Because analytics workloads ingest data from source systems, the owner of the source data should define the data classifications.
- [BP 3.3 – Understand data classifications and their protection policies](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.3---understand-data-classifications-and-their-protection-policies..md): Data classification in your organization is key to determining how data must be protected while at rest and in transit.
- [BP 3.4 – Identify the source data owners and have them set the data classifications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.4---identify-the-source-data-owners-and-have-them-set-the-data-classifications..md): Identify the owners of the source data, like business data owners, and agree what level of protection is required for the data within the analytics platform.
- [BP 3.5 – Record data classifications into the Data Catalog so that analytics workloads can understand](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.5---record-data-classifications-into-the-data-catalog-so-that-analytics-workloads-can-understand..md): Allow processes to update the Data Catalog so it can provide a reliable record of where the data is located and its precise classification.
- [BP 3.6 – Implement encryption policies](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.6---implement-encryption-policies.md): Data encryption is a way of translating data from plaintext (unencrypted) to ciphertext (encrypted).
- [BP 3.7 – Implement data retention policies for each class of data in the analytics workload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.7---implement-data-retention-policies-for-each-class-of-data-in-the-analytics-workload..md): The business’s data classification policies determine how long the analytics workload should retain the data and how long backups should be kept.
- [BP 3.8 – Enforce downstream systems to honor the data classifications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-3.8---enforce-downstream-systems-to-honor-the-data-classifications..md): Since other data-consuming systems will access the data that the analytics workload shares, the workload should require the downstream systems to implement the required data classification policies.

### [4 – Implement data access control](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-4.md)

How do you manage access to data within your organization’s source, analytics, and downstream systems?

- [BP 4.1 – Allow data owners to determine which people or systems can access data in analytics and downstream workloads](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-4.1---allow-data-owners-to-determine-which-people-or-systems-can-access-data-in-analytics-and-downstream-workloads..md): Data owners are the people that have direct responsibility for data protection.
- [BP 4.2 – Build user identity solutions that uniquely identify people and systems](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-4.2---build-user-identity-solutions-that-uniquely-identify-people-and-systems..md): To control data access effectively, the analytics workload should be able to uniquely identify the people or systems.
- [BP 4.3 – Implement the required data access authorization models](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-4.3---implement-the-required-data-access-authorization-models..md): User authorization determines what actions that a user is permitted to take on the data or resource.
- [BP 4.4 – Establish an emergency access process to ensure that admin access is managed and used when required](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-4.4---establish-an-emergency-access-process-to-ensure-that-admin-access-is-managed-and-used-when-required..md): Emergency access allows expedited access to your workload in the unlikely event of an automated process or pipeline issue.
- [BP 4.5 – Track data and database changes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-4.5-track-data-and-database-changes..md): Data auditing involves monitoring a database to track the actions of a user or process, and to audit the changes that have occurred to the data.

### [5 – Control the access to workload infrastructure](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-5.md)

How do you protect the infrastructure of the analytics workload? Analytics environments change based on the evolving requirements of data processing and data distribution.

- [BP 5.1 – Prevent unintended access to the infrastructure](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-5.1---prevent-unintended-access-to-the-infrastructure..md): Grant least privilege access to infrastructure to help prevent inadvertent or unintended access to the infrastructure.
- [BP 5.2 – Implement least privilege policies for source and downstream systems](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-5.2---implement-least-privilege-policies-for-source-and-downstream-systems..md): The principle of least privilege works by giving only enough access for systems to do the job.
- [BP 5.3 – Monitor the infrastructure changes and the user activities against the infrastructure](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-5.3---monitor-the-infrastructure-changes-and-the-user-activities-against-the-infrastructure..md): As the infrastructure changes over time, you should monitor what has been changed by whom.
- [BP 5.4 – Secure the audit logs that record every data or resource access in analytics infrastructure](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-5.4---secure-the-audit-logs-that-record-every-data-or-resource-access-in-analytics-infrastructure..md): Logs are an audit trail of events and should be stored in an immutable format for compliance purposes.

### [Reliability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/reliability.md)

The reliability pillar encompasses the ability of a workload to perform its intended function correctly and consistently when it’s expected to.

### [6 – Design resilience for analytics workload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-6.md)

How do you design analytics workloads to withstand and mitigate failures?

- [BP 6.1 – Create an illustration of data flow dependencies](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-6.1-create-an-illustration-of-data-flow-dependencies..md): Work with business stakeholders to create a visual illustration of the data pipeline.
- [Best practice 6.2 – Monitor analytics systems to detect analytics or extract, transform and load (ETL) job failures](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-6.2---monitor-analytics-systems-to-detect-analytics-or-etl-job-failures..md): Detect extract, transform, and load (ETL) and analytics job failures as soon as possible.
- [BP 6.3 – Notify stakeholders about analytics or ETL job failures](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-6.3---notify-stakeholders-about-analytics-or-etl-job-failures..md): Analytics and ETL job failures can impact the SLAs for delivering the data on time for downstream analytics workloads.
- [BP 6.4 – Automate the recovery of analytics and ETL job failures](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-6.4-automate-the-recovery-of-analytics-and-etl-job-failures..md): Many factors can cause analytics and ETL jobs to fail.
- [BP 6.5 – Build a disaster recovery (DR) plan for the analytics infrastructure and the data](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-6.5---build-a-disaster-recovery-dr-plan-for-the-analytics-infrastructure-and-the-data..md): Discuss with business stakeholders to understand maximum amount of data loss (RPO) and maximum amount of service loss (RTO).

### [7 – Govern data and metadata changes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-7.md)

How do you govern data and metadata changes? Controlled changes are not only necessary for infrastructure, but also required for data quality assurance.

- [BP 7.1 – Build a central Data Catalog to store, share, and track metadata changes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-7.1---build-a-central-data-catalog-to-store-share-and-track-metadata-changes..md): Building a central Data Catalog to store, share, and manage metadata across the organization is an integral part of data governance.
- [BP 7.2 – Monitor for data quality anomalies](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-7.2---monitor-for-data-quality-anomalies..md): Data quality is critical for organizations to accurately measure important business metrices, bad data can impact the accuracy of analytics insights and ML predictions.
- [BP 7.3 – Trace data lineage](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-7.3---trace-data-lineage..md): Have a clear understanding about where your organization’s data is coming from, how the data is transformed, who and what systems have access to the data, and how the data is used, is critical to increasing the business value of data.

### [Performance efficiency](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/performance-efficiency.md)

The performance efficiency pillar focuses on the efficient use of resources to meet requirements as demand changes and technologies evolve.

### [8 – Choose the best-performing compute solution](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-8.md)

- [BP 8.1 – Identify analytics solutions that best suit your technical challenges](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-8.1-identify-analytics-solutions-that-best-suit-your-technical-challenges..md): AWS has multiple analytics processing services that are built for specific purposes.
- [BP 8.2 – Provision the compute resources to the location of the data storage](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-8.2---provision-the-compute-resources-to-the-location-of-the-data-storage..md): Data analytics workloads require moving data through a pipeline, either for ingesting data, processing intermediate results, or producing curated datasets.
- [BP 8.3 – Define and measure the computing performance metrics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-8.3---define-and-measure-the-computing-performance-metrics..md): Define how you will measure performance of the analytics solutions for each step in the process.

### [9 – Choose the best-performing storage solution](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-9.md)

How do you select the best-performing storage options for your workload?

- [BP 9.1 – Identify critical performance criteria for your storage workload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-9.1---identify-critical-performance-criteria-for-your-storage-workload..md): In data analytics, throughput is often a constraining factor to enable your workloads to run effectively.
- [BP 9.2 – Evaluate and confirm the available storage options for your compute solution](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-9.2-identify-evaluate-available-storage-options.md): Many AWS data analytics services allow you to use more than one type of storage.
- [BP 9.3 – Choose the optimal storage based on access patterns, data growth, and the performance requirements](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-9.3---choose-the-optimal-storage-based-on-access-patterns-data-growth-and-the-performance-metrics..md): Storage options for data analytics can have performance tradeoffs based on access patterns and data size.

### [10 – Choose the best-performing file format and partitioning](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-10.md)

How do you select the best-performing file formats and partitioning? Selecting the best-performing file format and data partitioning for data-at-rest can have a large impact on the overall analytics workload efficiency.

- [BP 10.1 – Select format based on data write frequency and patterns for append-only compared to in-place update](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-10.1-select-format-based-on-data-write-frequency-and-patterns-for-append-only-vs.-in-place-update..md): Review your data storage write patterns and performance requirements for streaming and batch workloads.
- [BP 10.2 – Choose data formatting based on your data access pattern](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-10.2-choose-data-formatting-based-on-your-data-access-pattern..md): Choosing the right data type for your workload is important.
- [BP 10.3 – Utilize compression techniques to both decrease storage requirements and enhance I/O efficiency](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-10.3---use-file-compression-to-reduce-number-of-files-and-to-improve-file-io-efficiency..md): Store data in a compressed format to reduce the burden on the underlying storage host and network.
- [Best practice 10.4 – Partition your data to enable efficient data pruning and reduce unnecessary file reads](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-10.4---partition-your-data-to-avoid-unnecessary-file-reads..md): Storing your data in structured partitions will allow compute to identify the location of only that portion of the data relevant to the query.

### [Cost optimization](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/cost-optimization.md)

The cost optimization pillar includes the continual process of refinement and improvement of a system over its entire lifecycle to optimize cost.

### [11 – Choose cost-effective compute and storage solutions based on workload usage patterns](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-11.md)

How do you select the compute and storage solution for your analytics workload? Your initial design choice could have significant cost impact.

- [BP 11.1 – Decouple storage from compute](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-11.1---decouple-storage-from-compute..md): It’s common for data assets to grow exponentially year over year.
- [BP 11.2 – Plan and provision capacity for predictable workload usage](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-11.2---plan-and-provision-capacity-for-predictable-workload-usage..md): For well-defined workloads, planning capacity ahead based on average usage pattern helps improve resource utilization and avoid over provisioning.
- [BP 11.3 – Use on-demand instances or serverless capacity for unpredictable workload usage](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-11.3---use-on-demand-instance-capacity-for-unpredictable-workload-usage..md): Serverless services typically only charge for the compute used, or the use of other measures like data processed, but only when there is a workload actively using the service.

### [12 – Build financial accountability models for data and workload usage](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-12.md)

How do you measure and attribute the analytics workload financial accountability? As your business continues to evolve, so will your analytics workload.

- [BP 12.1 – Measure data storage and processing costs per user of the workload](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-12.1---measure-data-storage-and-processing-costs-per-user-of-the-workload..md): Data analytics workloads have recurring stable costs and per-use costs, for example, a weekly reporting job with relatively static data storage fees or periodic unpredictable processing runtime fees.
- [Best practice 12.2 – Build local or build centralized data analytics platforms](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-12.2-build-local-or-centralized-data-analytics-platforms.md): Teams can establish their own data analytics resources that support their analytical needs locally, rather than extracting information and transferring it to a central location.
- [BP 12.3 – Restrict and record resource allocation permissions using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-12.3---restrict-and-record-resource-allocation-permissions-using-aws-identity-and-access-management-iam..md): To better control costs, create distinct IAM roles that authorize users to provision certain resources.

### [13 – Manage cost over time](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-13.md)

How do you manage the cost of your workload over time? To ensure that you always have the most cost-efficient workload, periodically review your workload to discover opportunities to implement new services, features, and components.

- [BP 13.1 – Remove unused data and infrastructure](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-13.1-remove-unused-data-and-infrastructure..md): Delete data that is out of its retention period, or not needed anymore.
- [Best practice 13.2 – Continuously evaluate your provisioned resources and identify overprovisioned workloads](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-13.2---reduce-overprovisioning-infrastructure..md): Workload resource utilization can change over time, especially with the growth of data or after process optimization has occurred.
- [BP 13.3 – Evaluate and adopt new cost-effective solutions](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-13.3---evaluate-and-adopt-new-cost-effective-solutions..md): As AWS releases new services and features, it’s a best practice to review your existing architectural decisions to ensure that they remain cost effective.

### [14 – Use optimal pricing models based on infrastructure usage patterns](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-14.md)

How do you choose the financially-optimal pricing models of the infrastructure? Consult with your finance team and choose optimal purchasing options, such as On-Demand Instances, Reserved Instances, or Spot Instances.

- [BP 14.1 – Evaluate the infrastructure usage patterns and choose your payment options accordingly](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-14.1---evaluate-the-infrastructure-usage-patterns-and-choose-your-payment-options-accordingly..md): On-demand resources provide immense ﬂexibility with pay-as-you-go payment models across multiple scenarios and scales.
- [BP 14.2 – Consult with your finance team and determine optimal payment models](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-14.2---consult-with-your-finance-team-and-determine-optimal-payment-models..md): If you use reserved-capacity pricing options, you can reduce the infrastructure cost without modifying your workload architectures.

### [Sustainability](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/sustainability.md)

Sustainability in the cloud is a continuous effort focused primarily on energy reduction and efficiency across all components of a workload.

### [15 – Sustainability implementation guidance](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/design-principle-15.md)

Think about sustainability as being a non-functional requirement when designing your systems.

- [Best practice 15.1 – Define your organization’s current environmental impact](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.1-define-organization-current-environmental-impact.md): As an organization, you should track your progress towards your sustainability goals.
- [Best practice 15.2 – Encourage sustainable thinking](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.2-encourage-sustainable-thinking.md): Software architects are often encouraged to apply systems thinking to the problems they tackle.
- [Best practice 15.3 – Encourage a culture of data minimization](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.3-encourage-culture-of-data-minimization.md): Analytics relies heavily on large volumes of data being stored and processed.
- [BP 15.4 – Implement data retention processes to remove unnecessary data from your analytics environment](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.4-implement-data-retention-processes-to-remove-redundant-data-from-your-analytics-environment..md): The retention of data should be informed, relevant, and limited to what is necessary for the purposes for which the data is processed.
- [BP 15.5 – Optimize your data modeling and data storage for efficient data retrieval](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.5-optimize-your-data-modeling-and-data-storage-for-efficient-data-retrieval..md): How your data is organized in a data store, database, or ﬁle system can have an impact on the amount of resources that are required to store, process, and analyze the data.
- [BP 15.6 – Prevent unnecessary data movement between systems and applications](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.6-prevent-unnecessary-data-movement-between-systems-and-applications..md)
- [BP 15.7 – Efficiently manage your analytics infrastructure to reduce underutilized resources](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/best-practice-15.7-efficiently-manage-your-analytics-infrastructure-to-reduce-underutilized-resources.md): Ensuring your organization has the correct amount of resource provisioned for your workload is a diﬃcult and challenging task.


## [Scenarios](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/scenarios.md)

### [Data discovery](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/data-discovery.md)

Many organizations treat data like an organizational asset, meaning it is no longer the property of individual departments.

- [Characteristics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/characteristics.md): The common issue that can hold you back from maximizing the value of your data is the variety of data silos within your organization.
- [Reference architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/s1-reference-architecture.md): The following diagram illustrates the solution architecture and its key components for data cataloging, security, compliance, and data access requirements using DataHub.

### [Modern data architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/modern-data-architecture.md)

Organizations have been building data lakes to analyze massive amounts of data for deeper insights into their data.

- [Characteristics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/characteristics-1.md): Scalable data lake: A data lake should be able to scale easily to petabytes and exabytes as data grows.
- [Reference architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/reference-architecture.md)
- [Configuration notes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/configuration-notes.md)

### [Batch data processing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/batch-data-processing.md)

Most analytics applications require frequent batch processing that allows them to process data in batches at varying intervals.

- [Characteristics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/characteristics-2.md): Following are the key characteristics that determine how you should plan when developing a batch processing architecture.
- [Reference architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/reference-architecture-1.md)
- [Configuration notes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/configuration-notes-1.md): Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as a central metastore for your batch processing jobs, regardless of which AWS analytics service you use as a processing engine.

### [Streaming ingest and stream processing](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/streaming-ingest-and-stream-processing.md)

Processing real time streaming data requires throughput scalability, reliability, high availability, and low latency to support a variety of applications and workloads.

- [Characteristics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/characteristics-3.md): Scalable throughput: For real-time analytics, you should plan a resilient stream storage infrastructure that can adapt to changes in the rate of data ﬂowing through the stream.
- [Reference architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/reference-architecture-2.md)

### [Operational analytics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/operational-analytics.md)

Operational analytics refers to inter-disciplinary techniques and methodologies that aim to measure and improve day-to-day business performance in terms of increasing the efficiency of internal business processes and improving customer experience and value.

- [Characteristics](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/characteristics-4.md): Discoverability: The ability of the system to make operational data available for consumption.
- [Reference architecture](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/section-17.md)
- [Configuration notes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/analytics-lens/configuration-notes-3.md): As shared in the previous section, there are different options and a non-exhaustive list of tools that you can choose from to implement an operational analytics pipeline.

